<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01920</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9e61fff6-6707-4a98-a0a5-f7cd5030a5c0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>true statements about the graph that represents \(y=2x(x-11)\).  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> Its \(x\)-intercepts are at \((-2,0)\) and \((11,0)\). </item>
      <item> Its \(x\)-intercepts are at \((0,0)\) and \((11,0)\). </item>
      <item> Its \(x\)-intercepts are at \((2,0)\) and \((-11,0)\). </item>
      <item> It has only one \(x\)-intercept. </item>
      <item> The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is -4.5. </item>
      <item> The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 11. </item>
      <item> The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 4.5. </item>
      <item> The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5. </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer</emphasis>: Its \(x\)-intercepts are at \((0,0)\) and \((11,0)\). The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>equations whose graphs have a vertex with \(x\)-coordinate 2.  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\) </item>
      <item> \(y=(x-2)(x+2)\) </item>
      <item> \(y=(x-1)(x-3)\) </item>
      <item> \(y=x(x+4)\) </item>
      <item>\( y=x(x-4)\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x-1)(x-3)\), \(y=x(x-4)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Three different equations are given. Refer to these equations to complete questions a–f.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Equation</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-intercept</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-coordinate of the vertex</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=x(x-2)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>c. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>d. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-5x(3-x)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>e. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>f. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?    </item>
  </list>
      <list>
        <item>\((0,0)\) and \((0, 2)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,0)\) and \((2, 0)\) </item>
        <item>\((0,0)\) and \((0,-2)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,0)\) and \((-2,0)\) </item>
      </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0)\) and \((2,0)\)</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?  </item>
  </list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)?    </item>
    </list>
      <list>
        <item>\((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,-5)\) and \((0,4)\) </item>
        <item> \((5,0)\) and \((-4,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,5)\) and \((0,-4)\) </item>
      </list>


<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)? </item>
  </list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -0.5</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)?    </item>
  </list>
      <list>
        <item>\((0,0)\) and \((0,-3)\) </item>
        <item>\((0,0)\) and \((-3,0)\) </item>
        <item>\((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,0)\) and \((0,3)\) </item>
      </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\)</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)? </item>
  </list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1.5</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
   <item>What is the equation of the axis of symmetry of \(y = (x - 4)(x + 5)\)? (If needed, enter fractional values as a decimal.)
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -0.5</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer questions a–c about the function \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\).</item>
</list>
<list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\)?  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> \((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\) </item>
      <item> \((0,2)\) and \((0,4)\) </item>
      <item> \((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\) </item>
      <item> \((0,-2)\) and \((0,-4)\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para>
<list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Is \((-1,3)\) a point on the graph?  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> Yes </item>
      <item> No </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No. Substituting -1 for \(x\) gives \((-1-2)(-1-4)\) or \(-3 \cdot -5\), which is 15, so \((-1,15)\) would be a point on the graph, not \((-1,3)\).</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> Does the graph represent the equation \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\)? 
      
      <figure><media alt="Graph of nonlinear function, origin O. Horizontal axis from negative 8 to 6, by 2’s. Vertical axis from negative 8 to 12, by 4’s. Line passes through negative 7 comma 8, negative 5 comma 0, negative 3 comma 0 and 1 comma 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d7ede3e12cc95715ca6ed8c75079c4081888c1a"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>

    <list>
      <item> Yes </item>
      <item> No </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No</para></section>
</content>
